\subsection{Degradation Under Inboard-Diesel Engine Noise}
\label{sec:engine-noise-degradation}

The eight stationary v1.2.1 vessel-response records are replayed through
OU--II, OU--III and TFG with their ordinary sensor corruption plus a
sensor-path machinery-vibration model. The guard is disabled in the
degradation arms and enabled in Sec.~\ref{sec:engine-noise-guard}.
Every score uses the final \SI{900}{s}; pooling is the square root of the
mean squared record RMS. Filter parameters are fixed at the selected settings
across these arms; quality thresholds are unchanged.

\subsubsection{Vibration model}
The declared auxiliary is a three-cylinder four-stroke diesel on flexible
mounts, with a $2.6\!:\!1$ reduction gear and a three-blade propeller.
This is a prescribed vibration stress model applied to the 28 ft response,
not a measured engine/hull installation. It adds crank orders, driveline
and blade-rate lines, a broadband floor, governor variability,
accelerometer rectification and gyroscope acceleration sensitivity.
The mount transmissibility is
\begin{equation}
 T(f)=\sqrt{\frac{1+(2\zeta r)^2}{(1-r^2)^2+(2\zeta r)^2}},
 \qquad r=f/f_n .
 \label{eq:mount-transmissibility}
\end{equation}
The nominal cell uses \SI{2400}{rpm}, hull vibration RMS
\SI{0.60}{m.s^{-2}} and a two-pole \SI{80}{Hz} sensor bandwidth before
\SI{200}{Hz} sampling. The recorded vibration RMS is
\SI{0.4543}{m.s^{-2}}. The rigid-body RAO response and magnetic input remain
unchanged; encounter-frequency and propulsion-induced motion are outside
this experiment.

\subsubsection{Nominal cruise and sensor attribution}
Table~\ref{tab:engine-noise-cruise} reports the current paired measurements.
``Offset'' is the norm of the pooled per-axis mean-error magnitudes.
\begin{table}[t]
\centering
\caption{Pooled errors over eight vessel-response records at nominal cruise.}
\label{tab:engine-noise-cruise}
\footnotesize\setlength{\tabcolsep}{3.2pt}
\begin{tabular}{@{}llrrrr@{}}
\toprule
Family & Engine & 3-D [m] & 3-D offset [m] & Pitch [\si{\degree}] & Yaw [\si{\degree}] \\
\midrule
OU--II & off & 0.511 & 0.061 & 0.189 & 0.60 \\
OU--II & on & 18.166 & 6.440 & 4.899 & 103.77 \\
OU--III & off & 0.405 & 0.047 & 0.166 & 0.57 \\
OU--III & on & 27.258 & 6.775 & 2.092 & 103.88 \\
TFG & off & 0.416 & 0.089 & 0.083 & 0.55 \\
TFG & on & 9.574 & 8.820 & 7.545 & 105.29 \\
\bottomrule\end{tabular}\end{table}

OU--III's 3-D RMS rises from \SI{0.405}{m}
to \SI{27.258}{m}, a factor of
\num{67.36}.
At cruise the systematic component is \pct{24.9}
of the total RMS magnitude. Across families the offset fractions are
OU--II: \pct{35.5}, OU--III: \pct{24.9}, TFG: \pct{92.1}.
Static offset therefore does not dominate both OU families.
Pitch RMS and pitch offset are
\SI{2.092}{\degree} and
\SI{1.166}{\degree}.
The accelerometer update shares its residual between attitude, bias and
wave acceleration; a standing tilt can therefore leak gravity into the
horizontal reconstruction. This is a mechanism interpretation of the
paired ablations, not a source-uniform theorem.

Removing the modeled gyroscope perturbation changes nominal 3-D RMS by
at most \pct{0.020} across the families. The measured degradation is dominated
by the accelerometer path in this experiment.

\subsubsection{Speed, level and bandwidth}
The speed sweep is not monotone: all three families have local reversals.
The level and bandwidth controls should therefore be read directly from
Figs.~\ref{fig:engine-noise-speed} and \ref{fig:engine-noise-mechanism}.
The matched-power sweep holds recorded RMS at \SI{0.4543}{m.s^{-2}}
while changing bandwidth and folded order locations.
 For OU--II, the maximum/minimum 3-D spread falls from \num{20.89} to \num{4.71}.
 For OU--III, the maximum/minimum 3-D spread falls from \num{25.07} to \num{9.61}.
Recorded power explains much of the bandwidth sensitivity, while the
remaining spread shows that equal power does not make the inputs equivalent.

