\subsubsection{A front-end guard, and what it recovers}
\label{sec:engine-noise-guard}
\texttt{AccelVibrationGuard} conditions the accelerometer before the Mahony
proxy, MEKF and tilt watchdog. Two low-pass poles at \SI{14}{Hz} engage
using a separate two-pole \SI{25}{Hz} high-pass detector with
\SIrange{0.03}{0.08}{m.s^{-2}} rails and a \SI{5}{s} engagement time scale.
The covariance arm commands
$\sqrt{\sigma^2+(0.75 e)^2}$ from the same gated detector excess $e$.
These are fixed deployed settings. This study does not optimize the gain,
isolate forced-on quiet-input delay, or measure an engine-shutdown transient.

\begin{table}[t]\centering
\caption{OU--III vibration mitigation over the eight vessel records.
The last column is the guard plus covariance arm divided by the engine-off baseline.}
\label{tab:engine-noise-guard}
\footnotesize\setlength{\tabcolsep}{2.6pt}
\begin{tabular}{@{}lrrrrrrr@{}}\toprule
& \multicolumn{3}{c}{3-D [m]} & \multicolumn{3}{c}{Pitch offset [\si{\degree}]} & \\
\cmidrule(lr){2-4}\cmidrule(lr){5-7}
Condition & off & guard & $+R$ & off & guard & $+R$ & $\times$ base \\
\midrule
Engine off & 0.405 & 0.405 & 0.405 & 0.157 & 0.157 & 0.157 & 1.000 \\
\SI{800}{rpm} & 5.214 & 0.598 & 0.592 & 2.699 & 1.382 & 1.680 & 1.462 \\
\SI{1600}{rpm} & 19.770 & 0.512 & 0.470 & 4.837 & 1.479 & 1.104 & 1.160 \\
\SI{2400}{rpm} & 27.258 & 0.514 & 0.453 & 1.166 & 1.526 & 0.557 & 1.121 \\
\SI{3200}{rpm} & 31.342 & 0.669 & 0.471 & 6.295 & 1.634 & 0.579 & 1.164 \\
Quiet mount & 4.525 & 0.460 & 0.441 & 2.129 & 1.036 & 0.634 & 1.091 \\
Engine bed & 112.393 & 1.063 & 0.495 & 27.927 & 1.549 & 0.546 & 1.223 \\
Wide sensor & 27.794 & 0.458 & 0.440 & 1.224 & 1.055 & 0.036 & 1.087 \\
\bottomrule\end{tabular}\end{table}

The table reports conditioning and covariance inflation separately.
Their relative benefit depends on the engine condition; these measurements
do not imply uniform improvement from covariance inflation.
At \SI{800}{rpm}, pitch offset rises from \SI{1.382}{\degree}
to \SI{1.680}{\degree} with covariance inflation.
For all eight engine-off records the detector stays below its rail and
engagement is zero. Their guarded and unguarded scores are identical.
This finite-record transparency result does not establish transparency
for arbitrary seas, sensor noise levels or machinery spectra.

